{"author_count":2,"contributors":["Winther Pedersen, Joachim","Risi, Sebastian"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-382806"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["AI","Neuroevolution"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"evolving and merging hebbian learning rules increasing generalization by decreasing the number of rule","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-382806","ids":["itu-669b1c02-9834-4041-b4c2-b7a1ecdea11d"],"keyword":["AI","neuroevolution"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-4970A209EAAC66DF0AA1E5595BB87AAF"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Robotics, Evolution, and Art Lab","IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Design","IT University of Copenhagen, Play, Culture, and AI, Creative AI Lab"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Joachim","key":"wintherpedersenpau","last":"Winther Pedersen","locid":"ff6f398b-4bde-46da-a460-9815134b42eb","name":"Winther Pedersen, Joachim","orcid":"0000-0001-7884-9432","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Sebastian","key":"risipau","last":"Risi","locid":"a420ffb3-2ca6-4925-a25f-baaa5228fc15","name":"Risi, Sebastian","orcid":"0000-0003-3607-8400","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"status":"p"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-669b1c02-9834-4041-b4c2-b7a1ecdea11d"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Evolving and Merging Hebbian Learning Rules : Increasing Generalization by Decreasing the Number of Rule","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-382806","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"Applying neural network (NN) methods in games can lead to various new and exciting game dynamics not previously possible. However, they also lead to new challenges such as the lack of large, clean datasets, varying player skill levels, and changing gameplay strategies. In this paper, we focus on the adversarial player strategy aspect in the game iNNk, in which players try to communicate secret code words through drawings with the goal of not being deciphered by a NN. Some strategies exploit weaknesses in the NN that consistently trick it into making incorrect classifications, leading to unbalanced gameplay. We present a method that combines transfer learning and ensemble methods to obtain a data-efficient adaptation to these strategies. This combination significantly outperforms the baseline NN across all adversarial player strategies despite only being trained on a limited set of adversarial examples. We expect the methods developed in this paper to be useful for the rapidly growing field of NN-based games, which will require new approaches to deal with unforeseen player creativity.","author_count":6,"contributors":["Löwe, Mathias","Villareale, Jennifer","Freed, Evan","Sladek, Aleksanteri","Zhu, Jichen","Risi, Sebastian"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-384782"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","OECD"],"country":["United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Drexel University"],"keyword":["Adversarial Strategies","Ensemble Methods","Game Dynamics","Neural networks","Transfer learning"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"dealing with adversarial player strategies in the neural network game innk through ensemble learning","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-384782","ids":["itu-ee94392f-3fbf-417c-a7fc-21d45b1fd65f"],"keyword":["Adversarial Strategies","Ensemble Methods","Game Dynamics","Neural Networks","Transfer Learning"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-012B5D8A74E481D1DD7A22D9E421D2C6"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Robotics, Evolution, and Art Lab","IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Design","IT University of Copenhagen, Play, Culture, and AI, Creative AI Lab"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"us","key":"drexel university","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"Drexel University","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Mathias","key":"löwepau","last":"Löwe","name":"Löwe, Mathias","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Jennifer","key":"villarealepau","last":"Villareale","name":"Villareale, Jennifer","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Evan","key":"freedpau","last":"Freed","name":"Freed, Evan","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Aleksanteri","key":"sladekpau","last":"Sladek","name":"Sladek, Aleksanteri","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Jichen","key":"zhupau","last":"Zhu","locid":"e383647f-00d3-41b1-ad95-ff69bdfffb42","name":"Zhu, Jichen","orcid":"0000-0001-6740-4550","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Sebastian","key":"risipau","last":"Risi","locid":"a420ffb3-2ca6-4925-a25f-baaa5228fc15","name":"Risi, Sebastian","orcid":"0000-0003-3607-8400","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"status":"p"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-ee94392f-3fbf-417c-a7fc-21d45b1fd65f"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Dealing with Adversarial Player Strategies in the Neural Network Game iNNk through Ensemble Learning","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-384782","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"In games, as well as many user-facing systems, adapting content to users' preferences and experience is an important challenge. This paper explores a novel method to realize this goal in the context of dynamic difficulty adjustment (DDA). Here the aim is to constantly adapt the content of a game to the skill level of the player, keeping them engaged by avoiding states that are either too difficult or too easy. Current systems for DDA rely on expensive data mining, or on hand-crafted rules designed for particular domains, and usually adapts to keep players in the flow, leaving no room for the designer to present content that is purposefully easy or difficult. This paper presents Fast Bayesian Content Adaption (FBCA), a system for DDA that is agnostic to the domain and that can target particular difficulties. We deploy this framework in two different domains: the puzzle game Sudoku, and a simple Roguelike game. By modifying the acquisition function's optimization, we are reliably able to present a content with a bespoke difficulty for players with different skill levels in less than five iterations for Sudoku and fifteen iterations for the simple Roguelike. Our method significantly outperforms simpler DDA heuristics with the added benefit of maintaining a model of the user. These results point towards a promising alternative for content adaption in a variety of different domains.","author_count":3,"contributors":["González-Duque, Miguel","Berg Palm, Rasmus","Risi, Sebastian"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-382012"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Bayesian optimization","Dynamic Difficulty Adjustment","Gaussian processes"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"fast game content adaptation through bayesian based player modelling","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2105.08484","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-382012","identifier":[{"id":"85122939364","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-330da399-c53a-4398-af6d-f209b0ef5a71"],"keyword":["Bayesian Optimization","Dynamic Difficulty Adjustment","Gaussian Processes"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-9EE21E8B3BB1494D6B5DA16442842086"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2105.08484","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Robotics, Evolution, and Art Lab","IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Design","IT University of Copenhagen, Play, Culture, and AI, Creative AI Lab"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Miguel","key":"gonzálezduquepau","last":"González-Duque","name":"González-Duque, Miguel","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Rasmus","key":"bergpalmpau","last":"Berg Palm","name":"Berg Palm, Rasmus","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Sebastian","key":"risipau","last":"Risi","locid":"a420ffb3-2ca6-4925-a25f-baaa5228fc15","name":"Risi, Sebastian","orcid":"0000-0003-3607-8400","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1109/cog52621.2021.9619018","journal":"Proceedings of the 2021 IEEE Conference on Games","publisher":"IEEE","status":"p","title":"Proceedings of the 2021 IEEE Conference on Games","title_org":"Proceedings of the 2021 IEEE Conference on Games (CoG)"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85122939364"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-330da399-c53a-4398-af6d-f209b0ef5a71"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Fast Game Content Adaptation Through Bayesian-based Player Modelling","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-382012","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
