80d09d0"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Learning Abstract Forward Models","type":"Report","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-382622","year":"2019","yearsub":"2019"}
{"abstract":"Recent developments in memory-augmented neural networks allowed sequential problems requiring long-term memory to be solved, which were intractable for traditional neural networks. However, current approaches still struggle to scale to large memory sizes and sequence lengths. In this paper we show how access to an external memory component can be encoded geometrically through a novel HyperNEAT-based Neural Turing Machine (HyperNTM). The indirect HyperNEAT encoding allows for training on small memory vectors in a bit vector copy task and then applying the knowledge gained from such training to speed up training on larger size memory vectors. Additionally, we demonstrate that in some instances, networks trained to copy nine bit vectors can be scaled to sizes of 1,000 without further training. While the task in this paper is simple, the HyperNTM approach could now allow memory-augmented neural networks to scale to problems requiring large memory vectors and sequence lengths.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Merrild, Jakob","Rasmussen, Mikkel Angaju","Risi, Sebastian"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-382759"],"event":{"acronym":"EvoApplications","title":"European Conference on the Applications of Evolutionary Computation"},"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["HyperNEAT","Memory augmented neural networks","Memory vectors","Neural Turing Machine","Scalability"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"hyperntm evolving scalable neural turing machines through hyperneat","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/83703515/1710.04748.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-382759","identifier":[{"id":"85044071169","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-634992ae-decc-491f-b8bc-b4f28a8bc4e8"],"keyword":["HyperNEAT","Memory vectors","Memory-augmented neural networks","Neural Turing Machine","Scalability"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-8D26191936888695BA9CD2E489B6C245"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/83703515/1710.04748.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2018","yearSub":"2018"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Design"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Jakob","key":"merrildpau","last":"Merrild","name":"Merrild, Jakob","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Mikkel Angaju","key":"rasmussenpau","last":"Rasmussen","name":"Rasmussen, Mikkel Angaju","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Sebastian","key":"risipau","last":"Risi","locid":"a420ffb3-2ca6-4925-a25f-baaa5228fc15","name":"Risi, Sebastian","orcid":"0000-0003-3607-8400","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/978-3-319-77538-8_50","eisbn":["9783319775388"],"issn":["03029743"],"pages":"750-766","publisher":"Springer","series":"Lecture Notes in Computer Science","status":"p","subtitle":"EvoApplications 2018","title":"International Conference on the Applications of Evolutionary Computation"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85044071169"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-634992ae-decc-491f-b8bc-b4f28a8bc4e8"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"HyperNTM: Evolving Scalable Neural Turing Machines Through HyperNEAT","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-382759","year":"2018","yearsub":"2018"}
{"abstract":"Biological neural networks are systems of extraordinary computational capabilities shaped by evolution, development, and lifelong learning. The interplay of these elements leads to the emergence of biological intelligence. Inspired by such intricate natural phenomena, Evolved Plastic Artificial Neural Networks (EPANNs) employ simulated evolution in-silico to breed plastic neural networks with the aim to autonomously design and create learning systems. EPANN experiments evolve networks that include both innate properties and the ability to change and learn in response to experiences in different environments and problem domains. EPANNs’ aims include autonomously creating learning systems, bootstrapping learning from scratch, recovering performance in unseen conditions, testing the computational advantages of particular neural components, and deriving hypotheses on the emergence of biological learning. Thus, EPANNs may include a large variety of different neuron types and dynamics, network architectures, plasticity rules, and other factors. While EPANNs have seen considerable progress over the last two decades, current scientific and technological advances in artificial neural networks are setting the conditions for radically new approaches and results. Exploiting the increased availability of computational resources and of simulation environments, the often challenging task of hand-designing learning neural networks could be replaced by more autonomous and creative processes. This paper brings together a variety of inspiring ideas that define the field of EPANNs. The main methods and results are reviewed. Finally, new opportunities and possible developments are presented.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Soltoggio, Andrea","Stanley, Kenneth O","Risi, Sebastian"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-384681"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"country":["United Kingdom","United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Loughborough University","University of Central Florida"],"keyword":["Artificial neural networks","Evolutionary computation","Lifelong learning","Plasticity"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"born to learn the inspiration progress and future of evolved plastic artificial neural networks","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/83703601/soltoggio_nn18.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-384681","identifier":[{"id":"85051770903","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-e7cd3bec-3bb5-494a-b89f-b6903337f42a"],"keyword":["Artificial neural networks","Evolutionary computation","Lifelong learning","Plasticity"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-99606B3167F7D88779908A34EB11E521"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/83703601/soltoggio_nn18.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2018","yearSub":"2018"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"gb","key":"loughborough university","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"Loughborough University","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"us","key":"university of central florida","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"University of Central Florida","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Design"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Andrea","key":"soltoggiopau","last":"Soltoggio","name":"Soltoggio, Andrea","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Kenneth O","key":"stanleypau","last":"Stanley","name":"Stanley, Kenneth O","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Sebastian","key":"risipau","last":"Risi","locid":"a420ffb3-2ca6-4925-a25f-baaa5228fc15","name":"Risi, Sebastian","orcid":"0000-0003-3607-8400","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1016/j.neunet.2018.07.013","issn":["08936080","18792782"],"issn_org":["08936080"],"journal":"Neural Networks","pages":"48-67","publisher":"Elsevier","status":"p","title":"Neural Networks","volume":"108"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85051770903"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-e7cd3bec-3bb5-494a-b89f-b6903337f42a"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Born to learn: The inspiration, progress, and future of evolved plastic artificial neural networks","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-384681","year":"2018","yearsub":"2018"}
{"abstract":"Reward shaping allows reinforcement learning (RL) agents to accelerate learning by receiving additional reward signals. However, these signals can be difficult to design manually, especially for complex RL tasks. We propose a simple and general approach that determines the reward of pre-defined events by their rarity alone. Here events become less rewarding as they are experienced more often, which encourages the agent to continually explore new types of events as it learns. The adaptiveness of this reward function results in a form of automated curriculum learning that does not have to be specified by the experimenter. We demonstrate that this Rarity of Events (RoE) approach enables the agent to succeed in challenging VizDoom scenarios without access to the extrinsic reward from the environment. Furthermore, the results demonstrate that RoE learns a more versatile policy that adapts well to critical changes in the environment. Rewarding events based on their rarity could help in many unsolved RL environments that are characterized by sparse extrinsic rewards but a plethora of known event types.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Justesen, Niels","Risi, Sebastian"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-381485"],"event":{"acronym":"CIG","title":"IEEE Conference on Computational Intelligence and Games"},"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Automated Curriculum Learning","Exploration in RL","Rarity of Events","Reinforcement learning","Reward Shaping"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"automated curriculum learning by rewarding temporally rare events","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/83642393/Automated_Curriculum_Learning_by_Rewarding_Temporally_Rare_Events.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-381485","identifier":[{"id":"85056883493","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-0eaa5511-f8a3-4b04-88e6-85fb89437c0d"],"keyword":["Automated Curriculum Learning","Exploration in RL","Rarity of Events","Reinforcement Learning","Reward Shaping"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-D27F6560176880A57F225C83E5D8DE47"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1803.07131.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/83642393/Automated_Curriculum_Learning_by_Rewarding_Temporally_Rare_Events.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Design"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Niels","key":"justesenpau","last":"Justesen","name":"Justesen, Niels","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Sebastian","key":"risipau","last":"Risi","locid":"a420ffb3-2ca6-4925-a25f-baaa5228fc15","name":"Risi, Sebastian","orcid":"0000-0003-3607-8400","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"eisbn":["9781538643590"],"isbn":["9781538643594"],"pages":"293-300","publisher":"IEEE","status":"p","title":"2018 IEEE Conference on Computational Intelligence and Games"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85056883493"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-0eaa5511-f8a3-4b04-88e6-85fb89437c0d"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Automated Curriculum Learning by Rewarding Temporally Rare Events","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-381485","year":"2018","yearsub":"2018"}
{"abstract":"Plants are very efficient computing machines. They are able to sense diverse environmental conditions and quickly react through chemical and electrical signaling. In this paper, we present an interface between plants and machines (a cybernetic plant), with the goal of augmenting the capabilities of plants towards the creation of plant biosensors. We implement a data acquisition system able to stimulate the plant through different electrical signals, as well as record the electrical activity of plants in response to changing electrical stimulations, light conditions, and chemicals. The results serve as a proof of concept that sensing capabilities of plants are a viable option for the development of plant bio-machines. Different future scenarios (some speculative) are discussed. The work herein is carried out as a collaboration between the EU project Flora Robotica and the EU project NASCENCE.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Nichele, Stefano","Risi, Sebastian","Tufte, Gunnar","Beloff, Laura"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-382676"],"event":{"acronym":"IEEE SSCI","title":"IEEE Symposium Series on Computational Intelligence"},"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Cybernetic Plants","Electrical Signaling in Plants","Environmental sensing","Human Plant Interfaces","Plant Biosensors"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"towards a plant bio machine","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/82348910/nichele_PlantBioMachine.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-382676","identifier":[{"id":"85046078182","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-5de6d491-dab0-469e-b9d2-837248a24dad"],"keyword":["Cybernetic Plants","Electrical Signaling in Plants","Environmental Sensing","Human-Plant Interfaces","Plant Biosensors"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-539F53C7CB857B8DB7BD2DE09552445A"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/82348910/nichele_PlantBioMachine.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science, Machine Learning","IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Robotics, Evolution, and Art Lab"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Stefano","key":"nichelepau","last":"Nichele","name":"Nichele, Stefano","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Sebastian","key":"risipau","last":"Risi","locid":"a420ffb3-2ca6-4925-a25f-baaa5228fc15","name":"Risi, Sebastian","orcid":"0000-0003-3607-8400","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Gunnar","key":"tuftepau","last":"Tufte","name":"Tufte, Gunnar","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Laura","key":"beloffpau","last":"Beloff","name":"Beloff, Laura","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1109/ssci.2017.8285288","eisbn":["9781538627266"],"isbn":["9781538627273"],"pages":"1-8","publisher":"IEEE","status":"p","title":"IEEE Alife conference (SSCI) proceedings"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85046078182"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-5de6d491-dab0-469e-b9d2-837248a24dad"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Towards a Plant Bio-Machine","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-382676","year":"2018","yearsub":"2018"}
{"abstract":"An ongoing discussion in biology concerns whether intrinsic mortality, or senescence, is programmed or not. The death (i.e. removal) of an individual solution is an inherent feature in evolutionary algorithms that can potentially explain how intrinsic mortality can be beneficial in natural systems. This paper investigates the relationship between mutation rate and mortality rate with a steady state genetic algorithm that has a specific intrinsic mortality rate. Experiments were performed on a predefined deceptive fitness landscape, the hierarchical if-and-only-if function (H-IFF). To test whether the relationship between mutation and mortality rate holds for more complex systems, an agent-based spatial grid model based on the H-IFF function was also investigated. This paper shows that there is a direct correlation between the evolvability of a population and an indiscriminate intrinsic mortality rate to mutation rate ratio. Increased intrinsic mortality or increased mutation rate can cause a random drift that can allow a population to find a global optimum. Thus, mortality in evolutionary algorithms does not only explain evolvability, but might also improve existing algorithms for deceptive/rugged landscapes. Since an intrinsic mortality rate increases the evolvability of our spatial model, we bolster the claim that intrinsic mortality can be beneficial for the evolvability of a population.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Veenstra, Frank","de Prado Salas, Pablo González","Bongard, Josh","Støy, Kasper","Risi, Sebastian"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-384284"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Agent Based Spatial Grid Model","Deceptive Fitness Landscape","Evolutionary algorithms","Evolvability","Hierarchical If And Only If Function H IFF","Intrinsic Mortality","Intrinsic Mortality Rate","Mutation Rate","Senescence","Steady State Genetic Algorithm"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"intrinsic mortality governs evolvability","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1162/isal_a_00050","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-384284","ids":["itu-cdfc6ef3-ca56-4dd9-b3b6-f2b94054eaf8"],"keyword":["Agent-Based Spatial Grid Model","Deceptive Fitness Landscape","Evolutionary Algorithms","Evolvability","Hierarchical If-And-Only-If Function (H-IFF)","Intrinsic Mortality","Intrinsic Mortality Rate","Mutation Rate","Senescence","Steady State Genetic Algorithm"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-2-C35562CD3E62A8215513F064FCD69870"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1162/isal_a_00050","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/83658399/veenstra_alife18.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Design"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Frank","key":"veenstrapau","last":"Veenstra","name":"Veenstra, Frank","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Pablo González","key":"depradosalaspau","last":"de Prado Salas","name":"de Prado Salas, Pablo González","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Josh","key":"bongardpau","last":"Bongard","name":"Bongard, Josh","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Kasper","key":"stoypau","last":"Stoy","locid":"2a9c0934-98ca-4101-b829-b88d77f6487e","name":"Støy, Kasper","name_org":"Stoy, Kasper","orcid":"0000-0003-0179-2487","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Sebastian","key":"risipau","last":"Risi","locid":"a420ffb3-2ca6-4925-a25f-baaa5228fc15","name":"Risi, Sebastian","orcid":"0000-0003-3607-8400","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1162/isal_a_00050","pages":"242-249","publisher":"MIT Press","status":"p","subtitle":"ALIFE 2018","title":"Artificial Life Conference Proceedings"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-cdfc6ef3-ca56-4dd9-b3b6-f2b94054eaf8"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Intrinsic Mortality Governs Evolvability","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-384284","year":"2018","yearsub":"2018"}
{"abstract":"In April this year the programme chairs of EuroGP, the largest conference dedicated to genetic programming, Mauro Castelli and Lukas Sekanina, organised a panel discussion and debate about genetic programming with respect to the undoubted recent successes of other Artificial Intelligence techniques, particularly Deep Learning. The discussion was moderated by Prof. Wolfgang Banzhaf who holds the John R. Koza chair in genetic programming at Michigan State University in the USA. He started by inviting each member of the panel to give their position.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Machado, Penousal","O'Reilly, Una-May","Gori, Marco","Risi, Sebastian"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-384371"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Italy","Portugal","United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Massachusetts Institute of Technology","University of Coimbra","University of Siena"],"keyword":["Academic conference","Artificial intelligence","Deep learning","Genetic programming","Panel Discussion"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"nr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"eurogp 000000002018 panel debate genetic programming in the era of deep neural networks","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"djc","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-384371","ids":["itu-d35e182d-fee3-4a36-af67-a67a95b87213"],"keyword":["Academic Conference","Artificial Intelligence","Deep Learning","Genetic Programming","Panel Discussion"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-3-133782DC82A90B68F2D80D050B564302"],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"pt","key":"university of coimbra","loc":["Portugal","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Coimbra","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"us","key":"massachusetts institute of technology","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"Massachusetts Institute of Technology","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"it","key":"university of siena","loc":["Italy","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Siena","used":1},{"affno":"4","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Design"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Penousal","key":"machadopau","last":"Machado","name":"Machado, Penousal","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Una-May","key":"oreillypau","last":"O'Reilly","name":"O'Reilly, Una-May","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Marco","key":"goripau","last":"Gori","name":"Gori, Marco","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4","corresponding":null,"first":"Sebastian","key":"risipau","last":"Risi","locid":"a420ffb3-2ca6-4925-a25f-baaa5228fc15","name":"Risi, Sebastian","orcid":"0000-0003-3607-8400","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1145/3264700.3264701","issn":["19318499"],"issue":"2","journal":"ACM SIGEVOlution","pages":"3-6","publisher":"Association for Computing Machinery","status":"p","title":"ACM SIGEVOlution","volume":"11"},"review":"No Review","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-d35e182d-fee3-4a36-af67-a67a95b87213"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"EuroGP 2018 panel debate: genetic programming in the era of deep neural networks","type":"Journal Comment","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-384371","year":"2018","yearsub":"2018"}
{"abstract":"Bio-hybrid systems---close couplings of natural organisms with technology---are high potential and still underexplored. In existing work, robots have mostly influenced group behaviors of animals. We explore the possibilities of mixing robots with natural plants, merging useful attributes. Significant synergies arise by combining the plants' ability to efficiently produce shaped material and the robots' ability to extend sensing and decision-making behaviors. However, programming robots to control plant motion and shape requires good knowledge of complex plant behaviors. Therefore, we use machine learning to create a holistic plant model and evolve robot controllers. As a benchmark task we choose obstacle avoidance. We use computer vision to construct a model of plant stem stiffening and motion dynamics by training an LSTM network. The LSTM network acts as a forward model predicting change in the plant, driving the evolution of neural network robot controllers. The evolved controllers augment the plants' natural light-finding and tissue-stiffening behaviors to avoid obstacles and grow desired shapes. We successfully verify the robot controllers and bio-hybrid behavior in reality, with a physical setup and actual plants.","author_count":8,"contributors":["Wahby, Mostafa","Heinrich, Mary Katherine","Hofstadler, Daniel Nicolas","Risi, Sebastian","Zahadat, Payam","Schmickl, Thomas","Ayres, Phil","Hamann, Heiko"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-128626"],"filters":{"added":"2023-01","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Austria","Germany"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","University of Graz","University of Lübeck"],"keyword":["Bio hybrid systems","Machine learning","Plant Model"],"level":"sci","mra":["soc"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-ART","DI-U"],"level3":{"ART":["DI-ART-ALL","DI-ART-RDKA"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-ART-RDKA","DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"nr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"a robot to shape your natural plant the machine learning approach to model and control bio hybrid systems","src":["arkc"],"src-code":["DI-ART-RDKA"],"type":"dw","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://adk.elsevierpure.com/ws/files/62447386/Wahby_et_al_A_Robot_To_Shape_Your_Natural_Plant_arXiv_2018.pdf","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-128626","identifier":[{"id":"85050634013","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["arkc-eceb4dd5-1e91-420c-8147-e4174552364f"],"keyword":["Bio-hybrid Systems","Evolved Robotic Controllers","LSTM Network","Machine Learning","No","Plant Model"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Social Science"],"noi":["Local-1-1-E95B4A7B07342C940B98D5B35B6BC2BA"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://adk.elsevierpure.com/ws/files/62447386/Wahby_et_al_A_Robot_To_Shape_Your_Natural_Plant_arXiv_2018.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"de","key":"university of lübeck","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Lübeck","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"at","key":"university of graz","loc":["Austria","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Graz","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"dk","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT Universitetet, Games & Culture","IT University of Copenhagen, Center for Computer Games Research","IT University, Robotics, Evolution, and Art Lab"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"4","key":"royal danish academy architecture design conservation","l2":"DI-ART","l2n":"Artistic Higher Education Institutions","l3":"DI-ART-RDKA","l3nn":"Royal Danish Academy - Architecture, Design, Conservation","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Royal Danish Academy - Architecture, Design, Conservation","namevar":["Royal Danish Academy - Architecture, Design, Conservation, Royal Danish Academy - Architecture, Institute of Architecture and Technology","Royal Danish Academy - Architecture, Design, Conservation, Royal Danish Academy - Architecture, Institute of Architecture and Technology, CITA - Centre for Information Technology and Architecture","The Royal Danish Academy of Fine Arts, Schools of Architecture, Design and Conservation"],"nora_code":"DI-ART-RDKA","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Mostafa","key":"wahbypau","last":"Wahby","name":"Wahby, Mostafa","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Mary Katherine","key":"heinrichpau","last":"Heinrich","locid":"08c85002-6341-40a3-9046-b6222a08f965","name":"Heinrich, Mary Katherine","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Daniel Nicolas","key":"hofstadlerpau","last":"Hofstadler","name":"Hofstadler, Daniel Nicolas","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Sebastian","key":"risipau","last":"Risi","name":"Risi, Sebastian","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Payam","key":"zahadatpau","last":"Zahadat","name":"Zahadat, Payam","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Thomas","key":"schmicklpau","last":"Schmickl","name":"Schmickl, Thomas","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4","corresponding":null,"first":"Phil","key":"ayrespau","last":"Ayres","locid":"809e3672-ddc8-44e7-8a1b-1c1309974305","name":"Ayres, Phil","orcid":"0000-0002-9266-065X","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Heiko","key":"hamannpau","last":"Hamann","name":"Hamann, Heiko","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"pages":"15","status":"p"},"review":"No Review","search":{"DAF":["All Artistic Higher Education Institutions","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","Artistic Higher Education Institutions","DI-ALL","DI-ART","DI-ART-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["arkc-eceb4dd5-1e91-420c-8147-e4174552364f"],"src":["Royal Danish Academy - Architecture, Design, Conservation"],"title":"A Robot to Shape your Natural Plant : The Machine Learning Approach to Model and Control Bio-Hybrid Systems","type":"Working Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-128626","year":"2018","yearsub":"2018"}
{"abstract":"We propose the popular board game Blood Bowl as a new challenge for Artificial Intelligence (AI). Blood Bowl is a fully-observable, stochastic, turn-based, modern-style board game with a grid-based playing board. At first sight, the game ought to be approachable by numerous game-playing algorithms. However, as all pieces on the board belonging to a player can be moved several times each turn, the turn-wise branching factor becomes overwhelming for traditional algorithms. Additionally, scoring points in the game is rare and difficult, which makes it hard to design heuristics for search algorithms or apply reinforcement learning. We present our work in progress on a game engine that implements the core rules of Blood Bowl with a forward model and a reinforcement learning interface. We plan to release the engine as open source and use it to facilitate future AI competitions.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Justesen, Niels","Risi, Sebastian","Togelius, Julian"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-382831"],"event":{"acronym":"FGD(?)","title":"Foundations of Digital Games"},"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","Board Games","Game Engine Development","Reinforcement learning","Stochastic systems"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"blood bowl the next board game challenge for ai","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dca","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/83642386/justesen2018blood1.pdf","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-382831","ids":["itu-6841e3f6-92c6-47cf-bec0-f4a8d530e6bf"],"keyword":["Artificial Intelligence","Board Games","Game Engine Development","Reinforcement Learning","Stochastic Systems"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-BE7544311DCDA7A18AECFC7E3F5EF049"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/83642386/justesen2018blood1.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"http://tabletopgamesworkshop.org/papers/blood_bowl_the_next_board_game_challenge_for_ai.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Design"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Niels","key":"justesenpau","last":"Justesen","name":"Justesen, Niels","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Sebastian","key":"risipau","last":"Risi","locid":"a420ffb3-2ca6-4925-a25f-baaa5228fc15","name":"Risi, Sebastian","orcid":"0000-0003-3607-8400","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Julian","key":"togeliuspau","last":"Togelius","name":"Togelius, Julian","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"publisher":"Association for Computing Machinery","status":"p","title":"Blood Bowl: The Next Board Game Challenge for AI"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-6841e3f6-92c6-47cf-bec0-f4a8d530e6bf"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Blood Bowl: The Next Board Game Challenge for AI","type":"Conference Abstract","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-382831","year":"2018","yearsub":"2018"}
{"abstract":"Soft robotics is a growing field of research and one of its challenges is how to efficiently design a controller for a soft morphology. This paper presents a marine soft robot inspired by the ghost knifefish that swims on the water surface by using an undulating fin underneath its body. We investigate how propagating wave functions can be evolved and how these affect the swimming performance of the robot. The fin and body of the robot are constructed from silicone and six wooden fin rays actuated by servo motors. In order to bypass the reality gap, which would necessitate a complex simulation of the fish, we implemented a Covariance Matrix Adaptation Evolution Strategy (CMA-ES) directly on the physical robot to optimize its controller for travel speed. Our results show that evolving a simple sine wave or a Fourier series can generate controllers that outperform a hand programmed controller. The results additionally demonstrate that the best evolved controllers share similarities with the undulation patterns of actual knifefish. Based on these results we suggest that evolution on physical robots is promising for future application in optimizing behaviors of soft robots.","author_count":6,"contributors":["Veenstra, Frank","Jørgensen, Jonas","Risi, Sebastian"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-384325"],"event":{"acronym":"GECCO","title":"Genetic and Evolutionary Computation Conference"},"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Evolutionary Robotics","Evolutionary algorithms","Soft Robotics"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"evolution of fin undulation on a physical knifefish inspired soft robot","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"Andet","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/83217349/evolution_fin_undulation_postprint.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-384325","identifier":[{"id":"85050632454","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-d057c748-3cf6-41f5-beee-da2df6785955"],"keyword":["Evolutionary Algorithms","Evolutionary Robotics","Soft Robotics"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-A3E680F3FDDAD0B62B4EA167390C3694"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"Andet","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/83217349/evolution_fin_undulation_postprint.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Robotics, Evolution, and Art Lab"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Frank","key":"veenstrapau","last":"Veenstra","name":"Veenstra, Frank","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Jonas","key":"jørgensenpau","last":"Jørgensen","name":"Jørgensen, Jonas","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Sebastian","key":"risipau","last":"Risi","locid":"a420ffb3-2ca6-4925-a25f-baaa5228fc15","name":"Risi, Sebastian","orcid":"0000-0003-3607-8400","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Jennifer B.","key":"sartorpedh","last":"Sartor","name":"Sartor, Jennifer B.","rank":4,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Theo","key":"dhondtpedh","last":"D’Hondt","name":"D’Hondt, Theo","rank":5,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Wolfgang","key":"demeuterpedh","last":"De Meuter","name":"De Meuter, Wolfgang","rank":6,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1145/3205455.3205583","isbn":["9781450356183"],"place":"New York, NY. USA","publisher":"Association for Computing Machinery","status":"p","subtitle":"Genetic and Evolutionary Computation Conference, July 15–19, 2018, Kyoto, Japan","title":"GECCO ’18"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85050632454"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-d057c748-3cf6-41f5-beee-da2df6785955"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Evolution of Fin Undulation on a Physical Knifefish-inspired Soft Robot","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-384325","year":"2018","yearsub":"2018"}
{"abstract":"Deep reinforcement learning (RL) has shown impressive results in a variety of domains, learning directly from high-dimensional sensory streams. However, when neural networks are trained in a fixed environment, such as a single level in a video game, they will usually overfit and fail to generalize to new levels. When RL models overfit, even slight modifications to the environment can result in poor agent performance. This paper explores how procedurally generated levels during training can increase generality. We show that for some games procedural level generation enables generalization to new levels within the same distribution. Additionally, it is possible to achieve better performance with less data by manipulating the difficulty of the levels in response to the performance of the agent. The generality of the learned behaviors is also evaluated on a set of human-designed levels. The results suggest that the ability to generalize to human-designed levels highly depends on the design of the level generators. We apply dimensionality reduction and clustering techniques to visualize the generators’ distributions of levels and analyze to what degree they can produce levels similar to those designed by a human.","author_count":6,"contributors":["Justesen, Niels","Torrado, Ruben Rodriguez","Bontrager, Philip","Khalifa, Ahmed","Togelius, Julian","Risi, Sebastian"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-384860"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","OECD"],"country":["United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","New York University"],"keyword":["Deep reinforcement learning","Dimensionality Reduction and Clustering Techniques","Generalization","Neural networks","Procedural Level Generation"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"nr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"illuminating generalization in deep reinforcement learning through procedural level generation","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/84092128/illuminating.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-384860","ids":["itu-f4257e35-25e5-43de-b492-a2c6d4796ef7"],"keyword":["Deep Reinforcement Learning","Dimensionality Reduction and Clustering Techniques","Generalization","Neural Networks","Procedural Level Generation"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-08E6785D8114CFBB5FD8AAD939165BA0"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/84092128/illuminating.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Design"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"us","key":"new york university","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"New York University","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Niels","key":"justesenpau","last":"Justesen","name":"Justesen, Niels","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Ruben Rodriguez","key":"torradopau","last":"Torrado","name":"Torrado, Ruben Rodriguez","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Philip","key":"bontragerpau","last":"Bontrager","name":"Bontrager, Philip","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Ahmed","key":"khalifapau","last":"Khalifa","name":"Khalifa, Ahmed","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Julian","key":"togeliuspau","last":"Togelius","name":"Togelius, Julian","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Sebastian","key":"risipau","last":"Risi","locid":"a420ffb3-2ca6-4925-a25f-baaa5228fc15","name":"Risi, Sebastian","orcid":"0000-0003-3607-8400","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"status":"p"},"review":"No Review","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-f4257e35-25e5-43de-b492-a2c6d4796ef7"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Illuminating Generalization in Deep Reinforcement Learning through Procedural Level Generation","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-384860","year":"2018","yearsub":"2018"}
{"abstract":"We present potentials of creating excuses as a central metaphor for a design strategy - excuses to interact (a “ticket to talk”) as a way to facilitate social interactions among participants. Interaction design has, in its third wave, moved towards the more abstract experiential potentials of designing for embodied playful interactions, but much is needed to understand the dynamics of social interaction and how one can design for social curiosity. With this perspective we move beyond tangible curiosity, attempting to break restraint, timidity and shyness - using touch to design for bending social norms. We build the concept of creating excuses to interact based on three large experiments with interactive art installations presented in a festival context. We attempt to convert the particular design cases into general understandings of a set of potential design strategies, with the intention to create generative design knowledge. Extremely simple technical rules can be designed with the sole purpose of breaking down normal social restraints and provoking interesting social interaction.","author_count":10,"contributors":["Hobye, Mads","Padfield, Nicolas","Löwgren, Jonas"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-559912"],"event":{"acronym":"FGD(?)","end":"2018-08-10","place":"Malmö, se","start":"2018-08-07","title":"International Conference on the Foundations of Digital Games","year":"2018"},"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"country":["Sweden"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Malmö University"],"keyword":["Interaction design","Interactive art","Open ended play"],"level":"sci","mra":["hum"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-RUC"]},"level3all":["DI-U-RUC"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"designing excuses for interaction to facilitate social curiosity","src":["ruc"],"src-code":["DI-U-RUC"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3235765.3235828","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-559912","identifier":[{"id":"85055549943","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["ruc-3a52dd2b-5221-4331-a74e-907428fd3700"],"keyword":["Interaction design","Interactive art","Open ended play","Social curiosity"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Humanities"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-3-B87CD2D71CA64F0FCECB0F970887DFEF"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3235765.3235828","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ruc roskilde university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-RUC","l3nl":"Roskilde University","l3nn":"RUC Roskilde University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"RUC Roskilde University","namevar":["Roskilde University, Department of People and Technology"],"nora_code":"DI-U-RUC","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"se","key":"malmö university","loc":["Sweden","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Malmö University","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","country":"dk","first":"Mads","key":"hobyepau","last":"Hobye","locid":"51d04654-4c5d-4e18-a971-a46f7e61bd88","name":"Hobye, Mads","orcid":"0000-0001-8101-9982","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","country":"dk","first":"Nicolas","key":"padfieldpau","last":"Padfield","locid":"2030eff2-5745-4fae-bf22-9b6d425057bd","name":"Padfield, Nicolas","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Jonas","key":"löwgrenpau","last":"Löwgren","name":"Löwgren, Jonas","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Sebastian","key":"deterdingpedh","last":"Deterding","name":"Deterding, Sebastian","rank":4,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Mitu","key":"khandakerpedh","last":"Khandaker","name":"Khandaker, Mitu","rank":5,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"S